 ## Overview
-Blended-NeRF is a framework for editing a specific region of interest (ROI) in an existing Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) scene based on text prompts and a user-defined 3D ROI box [S1]. It solves the problem of local 3D editing by initializing a generator NeRF with the weights of the original scene and blending the synthesized content with the original scene using volumetric blending [S1]. Unlike methods that synthesize new objects from scratch (e.g., Dream Fields) or require semantic decomposition/2D masks for localization (e.g., Kobayashi, Benaim), Blended-NeRF operates on any region using a 3D box without learning a new feature space or requiring 2D masks [S1].
+Blended-NeRF is a framework for editing a specific region of interest (ROI) in an existing Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) scene based on text prompts and a 3D ROI box [S1]. It modifies the scene inside the box while preserving the scene outside, using a generator NeRF initialized with the original scene's weights and guided by a language-image model (CLIP) [S1]. Unlike methods that synthesize objects from scratch or require semantic masks, Blended-NeRF blends the synthesized content with the original scene using a novel volumetric blending technique that accounts for density and distance from the ROI center [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** An existing NeRF model $F_{\theta_O}$ representing scene $x_o$, a 3D ROI box $B$ localized inside the scene, and a text prompt $y$.
-*   **Output:** An edited NeRF model $F_{\theta_E}$ (implicitly defined by the blend of $F_{\theta_O}$ and $F_{\theta_G}$) that renders scene $x_e$ where $x_e \odot B$ matches the prompt and $x_e \odot (1 - B) = x_o \odot (1 - B)$ [S1].
+**Inputs:**
+*   Existing NeRF model $F_{\theta_O}$ representing scene $x_o$.
+*   3D Region of Interest (ROI) box $B$ localized in the scene.
+*   User-provided text prompt $y$.
+*   Hyperparameters: $\alpha$ (blending strength), $\lambda_T, \lambda_D$ (loss weights), $\tau$ (max transmittance), $\rho$ (max depth variance), $\epsilon$ (stability constant).
+
+**Outputs:**
+*   Edited scene representation $x_e$, defined by the blended radiance field.
 
